their original copyright notices and the Apache License, Version 2.0.

The packages eplansys_* are original contributions developed as part
of a terminal work at the Instituto Politécnico Nacional, Mexico, and
are also distributed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

The package plansys2_epistemic_planner contains, in its planning core,
code derived from the Aletheia epistemic planner: the Kripke state
representation, DEL product update, bisimulation contraction, formula
representation and model checking, heuristics, search strategies, the
selection policy, the parser and the validator. Aletheia is a work of
the same author and is incorporated here in process rather than invoked
as a binary; plansys2_aletheia_plan_solver runs that binary instead.

  Source:  https://github.com/HanielUlises/Aletheia
  License: Apache License, Version 2.0

The remaining files of that package - the plan solver plugin, the policy
plan serialisation, the action mapping and the formula text front end -
are original to this repository.

The package plansys2_epddl_grounder includes three files copied verbatim
from other projects, both used at run time rather than compiled:

  libraries/intermediate.epddl
    The "intermediate" EPDDL action-type library, from the plank
    toolkit by Alessandro Burigana and Francesco Fabiano.
    Source:  https://github.com/HanielUlises/plank
    License: MIT

  examples/muddy-children-domain.epddl
  examples/muddy-children-problem.epddl
    EPDDL sources from the epddl-workspace of Epistemic-Robotics.
    Source:  https://github.com/HanielUlises/Epistemic-Robotics
    License: Apache License, Version 2.0

The package plansys2_tui_cli vendors the Textual terminal UI library
under plansys2_tui_cli/vendor/textual, so that the command line front
end runs without a pip install into the ROS environment:

  plansys2_tui_cli/vendor/textual/
    The Textual TUI framework by Will McGugan and contributors.
    Source:  https://github.com/Textualize/textual
    License: MIT, reproduced verbatim in that directory's LICENSE file.

It is vendored unmodified and is excluded from this repository's linters
for that reason.

plank itself is not vendored. It is fetched and built from source
through dependency_repos.repos and invoked as a subprocess; its MIT
license applies to that build.

